WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.987
Rust normally enforces borrowing rules at
compile time.

2
00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:06.695
That is the preferred path because invalid
access patterns are rejected before the

3
00:00:06.775 --> 00:00:07.397
program runs.

4
00:00:07.477 --> 00:00:11.156
Interior mutability types exist for cases
where the design needs mutation through a

5
00:00:11.236 --> 00:00:14.863
shared structure and the rules cannot be
expressed with ordinary borrowing alone.

6
00:00:14.943 --> 00:00:18.622
`Cell<T>` supports simple
replacement-oriented patterns for copyable or movable

7
00:00:18.702 --> 00:00:19.065
values.

8
00:00:19.145 --> 00:00:22.669
It can be useful for small pieces of
internal state where the operation is limited

9
00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:23.785
and easy to understand.

10
00:00:23.865 --> 00:00:27.234
The important review point is that
mutation is still intentional and scoped,

11
00:00:27.314 --> 00:00:29.386
not a hidden workaround for unclear
ownership.

12
00:00:29.466 --> 00:00:31.747
`RefCell<T>` enforces borrowing rules at
runtime.

13
00:00:31.827 --> 00:00:34.677
It allows code to request shared or
mutable borrows dynamically,

14
00:00:34.757 --> 00:00:36.519
and it can fail if the rules are violated.

15
00:00:36.599 --> 00:00:40.174
That makes `RefCell<T>` useful for certain
single-threaded structures, tests,

16
00:00:40.254 --> 00:00:43.882
or graph-like designs, but runtime borrow
failures are operational concerns that

17
00:00:43.962 --> 00:00:45.568
should be considered during review.

18
00:00:45.648 --> 00:00:49.327
For threaded code, `Mutex<T>` and
`RwLock<T>` coordinate access through locking.

19
00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:52.257
These types are synchronization tools, not
only smart pointers.

20
00:00:52.337 --> 00:00:54.773
They can make shared mutation safe when
used correctly,

21
00:00:54.853 --> 00:00:57.703
but they also introduce questions about
lock scope, contention,

22
00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:01.514
failure handling, and whether a simpler
ownership model could avoid shared mutation

23
00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:02.060
entirely.

24
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.058
Interior mutability should be documented.

25
00:01:04.138 --> 00:01:06.729
Explain why compile-time borrowing did not
fit the design,

26
00:01:06.809 --> 00:01:09.970
what invariant the wrapper protects, how
long borrows or locks are held,

27
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.293
and what happens on failure.

28
00:01:11.373 --> 00:01:14.794
Defensive Rust uses these tools to make a
necessary pattern safe and explicit,

29
00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.620
not to hide a design that has become too
tangled to reason about.
